KNOWNBUG (real libstdc++ <tuple>).  std::make_tuple at arity >= 3 returns a
nondeterministic tuple, so std::get reads garbage.  Arity boundary confirmed
(2026-07-14): every arity <= 2 form passes (libstdc++ has a tuple<T1,T2>
partial specialization); every arity >= 3 make_tuple fails.  Direct
construction `std::tuple<int,double,char> t(1,2.0,'a')` now WORKS (fixed by
the defaulted-member/EBO-base-initializer work); only make_tuple's body is
lost.

ROOT (sharpened 2026-07-14, via temporarily disabling the system-header
error suppression): converting make_tuple's body fails with
  "found no match for symbol '__result_type'"
-- the return-type typedef
  typedef tuple<typename __decay_and_strip<_Elements>::__type...> __result_type;
does not resolve at arity >= 3.  The instantiation backtrace shows the tuple
constructor-constraint aliases (_ImplicitCtor / _ExplicitCtor / _TCC)
instantiated with argument types still containing UNRESOLVED __decay_t<T>
aliases (e.g. `const std::__decay_t<signed_int> &`), and their
__enable_if_t<FALSE, bool> guards wrongly evaluating FALSE.  The typecheck
failure is swallowed (system header), the body is dropped, and make_tuple
returns nondet.

Faithful hand replications all PASS (g++/clang++-verified): nested
alias-through-struct-typedef pack expansion (das<Es>::__type... with
das = struct { typedef strip<decay_t<T>>::__type __type; }), constrained
constructors via member alias templates over a constexpr member function
(TCC<C>::template ok<Us...>()), and their combination.  Two cvise attempts
with progressively sharper oracles (g++ compile+link+run plus the exact
swallowed-error signature) reduced to skeletons that clang++ rejects
(reduction artifacts), so the trigger is still tied to some further detail of
the real header.  Flip to CORE once make_tuple's return-type alias pack
resolves at arity >= 3.
